Let's go ahead and add a FileCreateCompleted event to the file-storage-adapter
Quick check before I add this — "Create" implies different semantics than the existing "Write" operation, and I don't want to guess wrong:

AskUserQuestion
OUT
The user answered: "What should distinguish FileCreate from the existing FileWrite operation?"="Same operation, just the FileCreateCompleted is only sent whan a file is created. It also is sent if a file is created by an outside service, say in the case of a remote shared directory.  FileWriteCompleted is sent when a file is written to or updated. We only need a FileWriteRequest operation. Implementation will automatically create files that do not exist.". Read the answers carefully — they may request clarification, changes, or that you not proceed — and follow what they actually say.
That reframes it nicely — it's not a new command, just a second possible outcome event for the existing FileWriteRequested command (auto-create-if-missing), plus the adapter also needs to notice files that show up from outside its own command handling (e.g. an external process writing into a shared/watched directory) and emit FileCreateCompleted for those too, with no preceding command or correlationId. That last part quietly pulls forward a narrow slice of the folder-watch capability that was otherwise deferred — worth flagging explicitly rather than letting it slide in unnoticed.

