OUT
Exit code 1
### 5. Denied publish (webhook-sender-01 has no publish list at all) ###
nats: error: nats: permissions violation: Permissions Violation for Publish to "events.files.FileWriteRequested"

### 7. Denied KV write (file-storage-01 -> a DIFFERENT adapter's registration key) ###
nats: error: context deadline exceeded
Different failure mode than the plain-pub case — a timeout rather than an explicit rejection. Let me confirm that's really the permission boundary and not a syntax issue, by trying the identical write with an identity that should have access:

Bash Confirm the same KV path succeeds for an identity with actual permission
IN
cd /home/bherring/dev/clodhopper/GregorsServiceBus
CREDS="infra/nats/operator/creds"

as() {
  local who="$1"; shift
  sg docker -c "docker run --rm --network gsb_default -v \"\$(pwd)/$CREDS:/creds:ro\" natsio/nats-box:0.19.7-nonroot nats --server nats://nats:4222 --creds /creds/$who.creds $*"
}

echo "### Same key, but as gsb-admin (has full \$KV.service-directory.> access) ###"
as gsb-admin kv put service-directory events.files.FileWriteRequested.some-other-adapter should-succeed 2>&1

OUT
### Same key, but as gsb-admin (has full $KV.service-directory.> access) ###
should-succeed
Confirmed — that's a real behavioral nuance worth documenting: KV write permission violations manifest as a timeout, not an explicit rejection, unlike plain pub/sub's immediate error. Now let's clean up test artifacts:

Bash Clean up test consumer, purge test messages, delete test KV keys
IN
cd /home/bherring/dev/clodhopper/GregorsServiceBus
CREDS="infra/nats/operator/creds"

as() {
  local who="$1"; shift
  sg docker -c "docker run --rm --network gsb_default -v \"\$(pwd)/$CREDS:/creds:ro\" natsio/nats-box:0.19.7-nonroot nats --server nats://nats:4222 --creds /creds/$who.creds $*"
}

as gsb-admin consumer rm EVENTS file-storage-01-consumer -f 2>&1
